Read `AGENTS.md` and `docs/INVARIANTS.md` before writing code. They are the
contract; this file is the short version of it.

## Rhythm

I build iteratively. Scaffold, play, revise, settle. Your job is to keep up with
that rhythm rather than to impose a plan on it.

- Make the smallest change that lets me see the thing working.
- Do not write planning documents, roadmaps, or phase breakdowns unless asked.
- Do not refactor code outside the change I requested. Note it in
  `docs/REPAIR-QUEUE.md` instead.
- Do not add abstraction until three real callers exist.
- Do not write tests during a spike. We backfill with `/backfill` once the shape
  stops moving.
- Keep diffs small. More than roughly six files touched means stop and check in.

## Marking rough work

When you knowingly write something exploratory, put a dated marker at the top
of the file or block with one line saying what is unfinished:

```ts
// SPIKE(2026-09-22): auth is faked until the shape settles
```

Use today's date. CI rejects spike markers on any pull request into the default
branch, and the harness starts nagging about a marker after thirty days, so this
is how rough code stays honest instead of quietly becoming permanent.

## Things the ratchet counts

The harness keeps a per-file count of these and will not let the total rise on
the way to main. Avoid them by default; when one is genuinely warranted, that is
fine, but know it is being counted: `any` and `as any`, `@ts-ignore`,
`eslint-disable` (if you must, add a reason after `--`), non-null assertions
(`foo!.bar`), and `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.

## Check your own work

Before you tell me a change is done, run `npx harness quick` and fix whatever it
reports. It takes a few seconds. Finding it yourself is better than me finding it
at commit time, and far better than CI finding it.

## Tell me, do not decide for me

Pause and ask before: adding a dependency, changing a schema, touching an auth
path, adding a background job or cron, sending data to a third party, or adding
an environment variable.

## Style

Write in the style already present in the file you are editing. Match its naming,
its error handling, its import order. Consistency with the surrounding code beats
your preferred idiom every time.
